<script module>
  import Panel from "$ui/components/Panel.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>CLI</h1>
  <p class="lead">One binary, <code>abide</code>, drives the whole lifecycle — scaffold, develop, type-check, build, serve, and package. Every command runs the SAME Bun pipeline (no divergent dev runtime), so what you see in <code>abide dev</code> is what ships.</p>
  <Panel id="create-a-project" title="Create a project">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide scaffold my-app
  # writes a starter project, then: git init + bun install + abide dev
  # skip any step: --no-git / --no-install / --no-dev</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>scaffold</code> writes a minimal, representative starter — one type-derived RPC, one page reading it, the middleware/lifecycle module, a config stub — then runs <code>git init</code>, <code>bun install</code>, and <code>abide dev</code> for you.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="develop" title="Develop">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide dev            # watch + rebuild + full live-reload (over the socket mux)
  abide dev --port 4321</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>dev</code> serves the app with a filesystem watch: on any change it rebuilds (Bun is fast) and signals a live-reload over the socket mux — a fast full reload, not stateful HMR (which would be a runtime that diverges from production).</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="type-check" title="Type-check">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide check           # type-check .abide (template + cross-file component props)
  abide lsp             # .abide language server over stdio (editors)</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>check</code> type-checks <code>.abide</code> files — script bodies, template expressions, and cross-file component props — by lowering to TypeScript and mapping diagnostics back. <code>lsp</code> exposes the same engine to editors (diagnostics, hover, go-to-definition, completion, signature help, references, and semantic-token highlighting) — the <code>packages/zed-abide</code> extension runs it in Zed.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="build" title="Build">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide build          # code-split client → dist/_app/&lt;hash&gt;/</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>build</code> produces the production client: a code-split, content-hashed bundle written to <code>dist/_app/&lt;hash&gt;/</code> with a stable <code>manifest.json</code> pointer. See <a href="/platform/deploy">Build &amp; deploy</a> for the output layout and how <code>start</code> serves it.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="serve" title="Serve">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide start          # serve the built dist (no bundler at boot)
  abide start --port 8080</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>start</code> serves the artifacts <code>build</code> produced — no bundler runs at boot. If no build exists yet, it builds once, then serves.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="run-a-script" title="Run a script">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide run scripts/seed.ts arg1 arg2
  # runs under the abide runtime (no HTTP; onStart / onStop still fire)</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>run</code> executes a one-off script under the abide runtime — no HTTP server, but the app's <code>onStart</code>/<code>onStop</code> lifecycle hooks fire, so migrations and seeds get the same environment as the server.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="tab-completion" title="Tab completion">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code># inside the binary's REPL, TAB already completes — nothing to install
  eval "$(./myapp completion bash)"     # bash
  eval "$(./myapp completion zsh)"      # zsh
  ./myapp completion fish | source      # fish</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>As you type at the prompt, dim ghost text appears inline: the best match where you are completing one, and once you have named a command, its whole signature — <code>--name &lt;string&gt; --loud</code>, with a boolean carrying no placeholder and an enum showing its set. Press <code>&rarr;</code> (or <code>ctrl-e</code>) at the end of the line to accept the flag itself, or keep typing to ignore it; it is never part of the line you submit, and it is suppressed under <code>NO_COLOR</code>. TAB completes command names, the reserved built-ins, a command's <code>--flags</code>, and a field's <code>enum</code> values — all read from your input schemas, so there is no completion list to maintain beside your handlers. The generated shell script does not bake those names in: it calls <code>myapp completion --line "&lt;line&gt;"</code> on every TAB, which is the same call the REPL makes, so the shell and the prompt can never offer different candidates and neither goes stale when a handler gains a field. Completing a command boots nothing.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="package-a-standalone-binary" title="Package a standalone binary">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>abide compile --target bun-linux-x64 --out ./server
  abide compile --platforms bun-linux-x64,bun-darwin-arm64
  # ONE executable: it hosts the app, dispatches your rpcs as subcommands,
  # and runs interactive — you pick when you RUN it, not when you build it
  
  abide bundle         # desktop launcher (host platform; embeds assets)</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>compile</code> emits a single self-contained executable (app + client assets embedded, no external <code>dist/</code>) — hosting it takes <code>--port</code> and <code>PORT</code> like <code>abide start</code>, and defaults its output to <code>dist/&lt;app name&gt;</code>. Everything the server would otherwise look up at runtime is resolved at build time: your rpc/socket/<code>app.ts</code> modules become static imports, and every page, layout and component is compiled to its server module ahead of time. The one thing a binary cannot carry is your SOURCE — a module that reads paths off <code>import.meta.dir</code> won't find them. There is no separate CLI build — the same executable is your command-line tool. <code>--platforms</code> cross-compiles a release set for the price of one client build. <code>bundle</code> builds a desktop launcher.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="your-app-as-a-command" title="Your app as a command">
    <pre class="code" data-lang="bash"><code>./myapp                        # interactive, schema-driven prompts (the default)
  ./myapp hello --who cli        # one rpc → JSON on stdout
  ./myapp help hello             # flags, typed from the input schema
  ./myapp serve --port 8080      # host the app in the foreground
  &gt; serve --port 8080            # …or from inside a session

  ./myapp connect https://app.example.com --token $TOKEN
  ./myapp hello --who remote     # …every later run goes there
  ./myapp disconnect             # …until you hand the app back

  ./myapp --url https://staging.example.com hello   # one run, somewhere else

  ./myapp login --token $TOKEN   # remember WHO you are there
  ./myapp identity               # …and ask the server who it thinks you are
  ./myapp logs --tail 200        # live log feed from wherever you point
  ./myapp logs --debug abide:rpc --trace 4bf92f35</code></pre>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p><code>logs</code> tails a deployment's log records as they happen. Filters are applied on the server: <code>--tail &lt;n&gt;</code> replays backlog before going live (200 by default), <code>--level &lt;l&gt;</code> sets a severity floor, <code>--debug &lt;pattern&gt;</code> takes the same grammar <code>DEBUG</code> uses, <code>--trace &lt;id&gt;</code> matches a trace-id <em>prefix</em> so the 8 hex a pretty line prints is paste-able, and <code>--no-follow</code> prints history then exits. Records arrive structured and are rendered by the READER, through the same formatter the server uses for its own stdout — so a remote tail at your terminal looks exactly like reading that server's console, and through a pipe degrades to the same <code>tsv</code>. At the prompt, ctrl-c detaches the feed instead of ending the session.</p>
    <p>It needs <code>ABIDE_LOGS</code> set on the <strong>server</strong>, and is a <code>404</code> otherwise. That default is deliberate: every other generated route discloses something the caller already holds, and this one discloses whatever your app logged — including lines written on behalf of other users. It is served inside your middleware chain, so authorization is your own middleware, the same answer abide gives everywhere else. Because the fan-out happens before the <code>DEBUG</code> gate, <code>logs --debug abide:rpc</code> can light a framework channel on a <em>live</em> deployment that was booted without it, which is the thing that makes a remote feed worth having.</p>
  </aside>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>The executable IS your app. Every rpc reachable by <code>clients.cli</code> becomes a subcommand, and its input schema becomes the flags — so <code>--n 5</code> on an <code>integer</code> field arrives as the number <code>5</code>, and <code>--help</code> knows the types without asking a server. Results are JSON on stdout (pretty at a terminal, compact through a pipe), a streaming handler is line-streamed as it arrives, and failures are a JSON object on stderr plus an exit code that names the failure class — <code>2</code> for a bad command line, <code>3</code> for a 422, <code>4</code> for 401/403, <code>7</code> for a 5xx.</p>
    <p>Where a call lands is a four-rung ladder: <code>--url</code> for this run, then <code>ABIDE_APP_URL</code> for this environment, then whatever <code>connect</code> stored for this user, and otherwise the app itself. Flag over environment over stored file, so <code>ABIDE_APP_URL=… ./myapp</code> beats a connected binary without disconnecting it first. <code>connect</code> keeps its target in your per-user data directory (mode <code>0600</code> — it may hold a bearer), which is why it survives between runs and follows you between directories; bare <code>connect</code> tells you where you are pointed, and both commands work from the prompt too.</p>
    <p>With nothing pointing it anywhere, it hosts its OWN copy of the app on a loopback port and calls that — offline, self-sufficient, and over real HTTP, so your middleware, identity, validation and caching behave exactly as they do in production. Hosting is lazy: printing help or mistyping a command never runs your <code>onStart</code>. With a URL it calls that deployment instead, authenticating with <code>--token</code> / <code>ABIDE_APP_TOKEN</code>; the embedded app still supplies the command table, so the CLI knows every flag even when the deployment is unreachable.</p>
    <p>Because the command surface is the DEFAULT, a deployment has to say <code>serve</code> — an entrypoint that omits it starts an interactive session with no console, which the binary reports as an error rather than exiting quietly.</p>
    <p>Nine names are reserved and win over an rpc of the same name: <code>serve</code>, <code>connect</code>, <code>disconnect</code>, <code>login</code>, <code>logout</code>, <code>identity</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>help</code> and <code>completion</code>. For <code>serve</code> the reason is rescue — hosting has no second door — but the rest are the binary's operational vocabulary <em>about</em> a deployment rather than calls into your app, and a reserved list you have to memorise exceptions to is worse than one shadowed rpc. Two more, <code>exit</code> and <code>quit</code>, are reserved at the <strong>prompt only</strong>: they end a session, which means nothing on a command line, so an rpc named <code>exit</code> is still callable as <code>./myapp exit</code> and is merely unreachable from the REPL. A shadowed rpc still appears in <code>help</code>, still runs over HTTP, MCP and the browser, and warns on the <code>abide:cli</code> channel naming which of the two you lost — or set <code>clients.cli: false</code> to drop it from this surface deliberately.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
